      "writtenOn": "2025-05-07T07:39:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My understanding is that the idea is to avoid updating caches for old changes: this would also impact new changes and therefore would cause a performance issue upon Gerrit restart as the cache will need to be recreated.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 103,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "fa3d331b08c3bb4a8c0ebf8e41eacc7530f1780d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b531f78c_12e078a6",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/pgm/Reindex.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 1004034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-05-07T10:29:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For our scenario we don\u0027t need to update caches at all since we run offline reindexing in a separate pod and when this finished we only copy the new index version to seed the bulk of the new index version into the gerrit primary pods. There we enable online reindexing with index.reuseExistingDocuments \u003d true` to catch up by only reindexing new or recently updated changes. Here we don\u0027t need to disable caches since the old changes have already been reindexed in the previous offline reindexing done in a dedicated reindexing pod.",
      "parentUuid": "dc65db83_6cd63171",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 103,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "fa3d331b08c3bb4a8c0ebf8e41eacc7530f1780d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55f14349_73f6cd33",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/pgm/Reindex.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-05-07T10:42:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we define a \"cutoff\" timestamp for updating the change cache, then it would also work in your case. E.g. You define a \"cutoff\" timestamp to _now +1day_ and therefore none of the changes updates will be persisted.\n\nThat would also work for other online cases, not only for offline reindexing.\n\nIn our case, we\u0027ll set to _now -1week_ and only the updates of the last week until now will trigger a change cache refresh.\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "b531f78c_12e078a6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 103,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "fa3d331b08c3bb4a8c0ebf8e41eacc7530f1780d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd645a13_e1de2aeb",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/pgm/Reindex.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 1065256
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-05-07T10:49:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e this would also impact new changes and therefore would cause a performance issue upon Gerrit restart as the cache will need to be recreated.\n\nI don\u0027t believe this will be the case. Reindexing the data should not lead to updated caches, if the entries already exist. I believe while coinciding updates to an index field and a cached object might happen, e.g. a new parameter in the change metadata that can also be used to query changes, these updates should still be decoupled. While reindexing would update the cache with the updated objects, it can slow down reindexing a lot, which also might affect users and during online reindexing user usage of Gerrit would still be slower, since a lot of cache entries would have to be recreated. Also huge caches (in our case 100s of Gb will cause other performance issues as well. I believe, if we would want a way to update caches with a new object schema or prefill them, this should be an independent task.\n\n\u003e If we define a \"cutoff\" timestamp for updating the change cache, then it would also work in your case.\n\nYes, that might work and would be a possible optimization even outside of reindexing to keep caches lean. In our scenario, I would still prefer completely read-only caches (see Matthias\u0027 explanation), but I would be open to implement that on top of it. Would that work for you?",
      "parentUuid": "dc65db83_6cd63171",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 103,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "fa3d331b08c3bb4a8c0ebf8e41eacc7530f1780d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1afe1295_80ba7c85",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/pgm/Reindex.java",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
